   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.3. Increased capacity of member States to develop gender-responsive legislation, policies and measures for a world of work free from violence and harassment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The revised Labour law approved in July 2021 includes important provisions in relation to the protection from and prohibition of violence and harassment.  

In addition, and with a view to provide tailored response to this phenomenon, the National Human Rights Commission of Mongolia (NHRCM) is undertaking a situation analysis of workplace harassment and violence in both private and public sector. The analysis, which includes the development of a questionnaire and a survey to assess the prevalence of violence and harassment, will be finalised by the end of 2021. 

Both employers and workers&apos; organisations took measures to strengthen the capacity of their members in addressing issues related to violence and harassment. This has resulted in enhanced awareness on the topic and a strengthened capacity to tackle the issue at the workplace level. In particular, the Confederation of Mongolian Trade Unions (CMTU) has been developing a violence and harassment internal policy and a training module on C190. All these materials will be finalized by the end of October 2021.  

While the violence and harassment policy will serve as an internal policy document, the training module will be used to strengthen the capacity of CMTU&apos;s members. In particular, CMTU will train targeted 120 leaders/members on C190 by using the training module by November 2021. Moreover, the CMTU will organise a public discussion on violence and harassment in November 2021. The Mongolian Employers&apos; Federation has produced awareness raising materials on issues related to the prevention of and protection from violence and harassment, and is disseminating them to 8,000 business entities. ILO has provided support to revise the Labour Law of 1999 since 2012. In particular, the new Code benefitted from a thorough and continued ILO&apos;s support, both in the field and in headquarter. The ILO is also currently supporting a gap analysis of the legal and policy frameworks vis-a-vis C190, to assess whether other amendments in different legislations may be needed. 

ILO supported the capacity development on C190 and R206 of tripartite constituents. Five representatives of Ministry of Labour and Social Protection (MLSP), Confederation of Mongolian Trade Unions (CMTU), Mongolian Employers&apos; Federation (MONEF), National Human Rights Commission of Mongolia (NHRCM) and National Committee on Gender Equality (NCGE) participated in the ITC Turin course on &quot;Ending Violence and Harassment in the World of Work&quot; from 26th of April to 4th of June. The participants co-shared highlights with peers, and knowledge-sharing opportunities were created to cross-fertilize lessons learned during the course. In addition, the ILO widely disseminated information on the C190 Global Action Week (21-25 June 2021) through ILO Mongolia&apos;s social media channel. The Office is also translating and localizing the C190 global campaign materials into Mongolian to strengthen advocacy and further raise awareness on issues related to violence and harassment, and C190 in particular. 

ILO is currently providing technical support to NHRCM in undertaking the situational analysis of work-related violence and harassment in both private and public sectors.</narrative>
   </description>
